#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-05.png", width: 202pt), 201.5pt, 599pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.9pt, 790.176pt, "5 ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 15.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#2f5496"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 87.26pt, "1")
  #line-at(93.624pt, 87.26pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.58pt, "Nucleate pool boiling is a complex liquid-to-vapour phase-change phenomenon that is associated with ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 117.98pt, "exceptionally high heat transfer rates at relatively low and stable temperatures. These appealing ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 132.5pt, "characteristics have led to nucleate pool boiling being used in a wide array of engineering applications ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 147.02pt, "from electricity generation to the cooling of micro-electronics. It has also been the subject of a vast ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 161.54pt, "amount of research, with pioneering work done by Nukiyama [3], who developed the first boiling curve ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 175.94pt, "for water at atmospheric pressure. Since then, the complexity of nucleate pool boiling has been ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 190.46pt, "exhibited as it has been shown to be dependent on many factors including pressure, wall superheat, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 204.98pt, "fluid properties, and surface geometry. Many studies have been devoted to investigating various forms ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 219.5pt, "of surface enhancement for the purposes of increasing the heat transfer coefficients. The use of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 233.9pt, "cavities, pin-fins, microchannels, surface roughness, porous structures, porous coatings, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.42pt, "wettability patterns have all been investigated and shown to offer potential improvements to the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 262.97pt, "boiling heat transfer coefficient [4, 5]. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 285.41pt, "Cavities and cavity arrays offer a structured, replicable, and relatively simple way to enhance a boiling ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 299.93pt, "surface. They enhance the boiling heat transfer coefficient through several ways, one of which is the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 314.45pt, "promotion of bubble nucleation by means of vapour entrapment. The presence of tiny vapour ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 328.97pt, "bubbles/nuclei entrapped by cavities, pores, or scratches on the heated surface encourages bubble ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 343.37pt, "nucleation as the nuclei can merge to form a bubble, which is then able to grow. The entrapment ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 357.89pt, "potential of conical cavities was first studied by Bankoff [6], who proposed that, for a given wetting ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 372.41pt, "fluid with a static contact angle of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(226.01pt, 372.41pt, "𝜃")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(232.25pt, 372.41pt, ", a conical cavity is capable of sufficiently entrapping vapour such ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 386.93pt, "that nucleation is favoured when the conical cavity angle (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(333.31pt, 386.93pt, "𝜃")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(339.55pt, 387.89pt, "cav")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(349.15pt, 386.93pt, " as indicated in Figure 1(b)) is less than ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 401.33pt, "half the contact angle, i.e. ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(190.37pt, 401.33pt, "𝜃")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(196.61pt, 402.29pt, "cav")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(206.21pt, 401.33pt, " < ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(216.65pt, 401.33pt, "𝜃/2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(234.41pt, 401.33pt, ". Lorenz [7] came to the same conclusion as Bankoff [6], whereas ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 415.85pt, "Tong et al. [8] proposed using the dynamic advancing contact angle as opposed to the static contact ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 430.37pt, "angle. Other researchers developed alternative criteria for vapour entrapment inside conical cavities ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 444.91pt, "[9, 10], but the review of Mahmoud and Karayiannis [4] highlighted that the Bankoff [6] criterion is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 459.31pt, "sufficiently conservative and appropriate for engineering design. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 481.87pt, "If a cavity is capable of entrapping vapour (thus satisfying the Bankoff [6] criterion), a bubble may ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 496.39pt, "nucleate within this cavity if the wall superheat is sufficient. The required superheat to initiate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 510.79pt, "nucleation has been shown to be related to the cavity radius [2]. Griffith and Wallis [11] did pioneering ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 525.31pt, "work in modelling the relationship between cavity size and the required superheat for bubble ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 539.83pt, "nucleation. However, their model was limited as it predicted a single active cavity radius for a given ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 554.35pt, "wall superheat, whereas Hsu [2] noted that for a given wall superheat, the active nucleation sites have ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 568.75pt, "a range of radii. Hsu [2] developed a model to calculate the range of active cavity radii (")
