// arXiv source page 6
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-06.png", width: 127.5pt), 235pt, 73.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.9pt, 790.176pt, "6 ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(77.424pt, 260.66pt, "Figure 1: Schematic of (a) cylindrical and (b) conical cavity geometries indicating cavity depth, radius, and angle. The blue ")
  #line-at(77.304pt, 271.73pt, "area represents vapour within the cavity, yellow area represents the bubble volume that has emerged from the cavity but ")
  #line-at(180.86pt, 282.77pt, "not detached, and the green area represents a detached bubble. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 305.57pt, "Shoji and Takagi [12] experimentally investigated the effect of conical, cylindrical, and re-entrant ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 319.97pt, "cavities created on a copper plate on the single bubble dynamics of saturated water. They found that ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 334.49pt, "both cylindrical and re-entrant cavities exhibited higher stability. Conical cavities were prone to periods ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 349.01pt, "of inactivity and required high superheat to maintain bubbling, although it should be noted that their ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 363.53pt, "conical cavities had large cavity angles of approximately 59° and 63.5°.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 385.97pt, "Narayan et al. [13] investigated the effect of cylindrical cavity depth on the bubble dynamics of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 400.49pt, "saturated water on an aluminium plate. Although the cavity depth had a negligible impact on the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 414.89pt, "bubble departure diameter, it affected the departure frequency with shallower cavities leading to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 429.41pt, "earlier departure. The departure frequency was shown to be consistent for deeper cavities, but as the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 443.95pt, "level of superheat increased, variation in departure frequency increased with shallower cavities ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 458.47pt, "resulting in significantly more deviation. In addition, for deep cavities, the maximum bubble base ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 472.87pt, "diameter was independent of superheat, whereas the more rapid and chaotic growth from shallower ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 487.39pt, "cavities resulted in the maximum bubble base diameter varying with surface superheat. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 509.95pt, "The impact of conical cavity angle has also recently been investigated by Narayan et al. [14] for conical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 524.35pt, "cavities with a constant depth. Comparing with the results from a cylindrical cavity with similar ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 538.87pt, "dimensions, it was shown that bubble departure diameter was similar for cavities with similar ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 553.39pt, "diameters, regardless of their shape. It was also shown that conical cavities with larger cavity angles ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 567.91pt, "resulted in longer bubble growth periods.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 590.35pt, "Apart from the work done by the groups of Shoji et al. [12, 15, 16] and Narayan et al. [13, 14], little ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 604.87pt, "literature could be found detailing the influence of cavity geometry on the bubble dynamics of single ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 619.42pt, "bubble nucleate pool boiling. While these studies have provided insight into the influence of cavity ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 633.82pt, "geometry, the range and resolution of cavity depths and radii investigated have been limited. Although ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 648.34pt, "it is experimentally challenging to manufacture and test a wide array of cavity sizes, it can be more ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 662.86pt, "efficiently investigated numerically. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 685.3pt, "In recent years, the volume of fluid (VOF) method has seen much use in the numerical study of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 699.82pt, "nucleate pool boiling. Influential work was done by the group of Stephen and Kunkelmann [17-19], ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 714.34pt, "who developed a sub-model to account for microlayer evaporation [20]. They implemented the phase ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 728.86pt, "change model developed by Hardt and Wondra [21] which was developed for both VOF and level set ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 743.256pt, "(LS) applications and is now a standard model in phase change literature. Sato and Ničeno [22] utilised ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 757.776pt, "an equivalent method to VOF that they refer to as a “colour function” to develop a depletable ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(318.55pt, 152.54pt, "𝜃")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324.43pt, 154.82pt, "𝐶𝑎𝑣")
