#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-07.png", width: 146.5pt), 97pt, 510pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.9pt, 790.176pt, "7 ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.44pt, "microlayer model [23] and simulate nucleate pool boiling from the single bubble regime at low heat ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 96.98pt, "fluxes, up to the “vapour mushroom” regime at higher heat fluxes [24]. The model of Sato and Ničeno ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 111.5pt, "[22] has shown good agreement with experimental data and the capability to model the transition ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 125.9pt, "between boiling regimes [25].  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 148.46pt, "The VOF method is capable of accurately modelling nucleate pool boiling under a variety of conditions ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 162.86pt, "while including evaporation from the microlayer and conjugate heat transfer. Despite its pervasive use, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 177.38pt, "few studies could be found which utilised the VOF method to investigate the influence of surface ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 191.9pt, "geometry on nucleate pool boiling. With limited information regarding the fundamentals of how the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 206.42pt, "cavity geometry impacts bubble dynamics, the design and optimisation of boiling surfaces becomes ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 220.82pt, "difficult. Furthermore, as will be shown in this paper, existing correlations cannot accurately predict ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 235.34pt, "the bubble dynamics from different cavity geometries. Hence, the purpose of this study was to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 249.86pt, "numerically investigate the influence of cylindrical and conical cavity geometries on the bubble ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 264.41pt, "dynamics of a single bubble under nucleate pool boiling conditions. A wide array of cavity radii, depths ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 278.81pt, "and angles were investigated to identify critical cavity radii as well suggestions to optimise rewetting ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 293.33pt, "heat transfer. ")
