#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-12.png", width: 451.5pt), 72pt, 427pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(512.26pt, 790.176pt, "12 ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.44pt, "While the chosen cavity dimensions are comparable to previous studies [13, 14, 36], the large cavity ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 96.98pt, "angles of some conical cavities could make nucleation unlikely, therefore the Hsu model (Equation 1) ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 111.5pt, "was used to verify that nucleation can be expected. For the cylindrical cavities at a superheat of 6.26 K ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 125.9pt, "(taken from validation case [36]), the Hsu model predicted nucleation in cavities with radii ranging ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 140.42pt, "from 0.06 μm to 651.22 μm, implying nucleation should be possible for all cylindrical cavities ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 154.94pt, "considered in this study. For the conical cavity with a cavity angle of 76° (the worst-case scenario), the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 169.46pt, "model predicted nucleation in cavities with radii ranging from 0.10 μm to 142.64 μm, implying that ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 183.86pt, "nucleation is unlikely in some of the conical cavities. However, the cavities with radii falling outside the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 198.38pt, "range of the Hsu model remained part of the test matrix with the rationale being that modern surface ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 212.9pt, "treatments can be utilised to create sub-cavities or nanopores that may provide nucleation points ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 227.42pt, "within the cavities, similar to the work of Deng et al. [37]. Explorations into how nanopore nucleation ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 241.82pt, "may be incorporated in micro-cavities may be achieved using Molecular Dynamics [38, 39]. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#2f5496"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 266.45pt, "2.4")
  #line-at(100.82pt, 266.45pt, "Calculation Domain and Mesh Independence ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 281.45pt, "The domain and mesh formulation presented in Figure 2 contained four zones to allow for variable ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 295.97pt, "mesh refinement. The mesh was 2D axisymmetric, encompassing a 5° segment centred at the ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(495.82pt, 295.97pt, "y")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(500.74pt, 295.97pt, "-axis. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.49pt, "The impact of the domain size on the bubble departure diameter and departure time was investigated ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 325.01pt, "using the departure metrics associated with the smallest fluid domain as a reference. When comparing ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 339.41pt, "the results in Table II, it follows that the bubble departure metrics were negligibly impacted by the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 353.93pt, "domain size, all being within 1% of the reference. Therefore, similar to the study of Li et al. [34], the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 368.45pt, "fluid domain with a height of 5 mm and a width of 2.52 mm was selected. The solid domain had a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 382.97pt, "height of 3.52 mm which had a negligible influence on the heat transfer in this system. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.144pt, 403.61pt, "Table II: Comparison of bubble departure diameter and time using different domain radii and domain heights. The departure ")
  #line-at(162.38pt, 414.65pt, "metrics associated with the smallest fluid domain was used as a reference. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
