  #line-at(72.024pt, 478.99pt, "Figure 7, show the effect of cavity radius on the departure diameter for both cylindrical and conical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 493.51pt, "cavities. Figure 7(a) shows that for narrow cylindrical cavities (radius of 50 μm and 100 μm), there was ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 507.91pt, "negligible difference in their respective departure diameters. Once a critical cavity radius was ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 522.43pt, "exceeded, larger bubbles were attainable with an increase in cavity radius. The same trend is observed ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 536.95pt, "in Figure 7(b) for bubbles departing from conical cavities, although for larger cavity angles the trend ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 551.47pt, "deviates with bubbles being smaller at departure. The three-dimensional summary of the effect of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 565.87pt, "cavity geometry on the departure diameter in Figure 8 better represents this discontinuous behaviour. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 580.39pt, "The narrow cavities had a constant departure diameter of about 900 μm. Once the cavity radius ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 594.91pt, "increased beyond 100 μm, the departure diameter increased linearly with the cavity radius. The ")
