  #line-at(223.97pt, 433.61pt, "w")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(228.89pt, 432.65pt, ") between bubble departure and the nucleation of the subsequent ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 447.19pt, "bubble, allowing for higher bubble departure frequencies and heat transfer rates. If all the vapour ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 461.71pt, "detaches from the surface, the thermal boundary layer must develop and a new bubble must nucleate, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 476.23pt, "which increases the waiting time. Due to the absence of bubble growth during this waiting time, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 490.63pt, "heat transfer rate decreases and the possibility of overheating the surface, and damage to components ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 505.15pt, "in the case of electronic immersion cooling, increases. Therefore, minimising waiting times is ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 519.67pt, "important in ensuring the longevity of a system and should be taken into consideration for cavity array ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 534.19pt, "designs.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 556.63pt, "While larger cavity angles may result in more surface rewetting and larger bubble departure diameters, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 571.15pt, "the Bankoff [6] criterion is recommended for the design of conical cavity arrays to ensure cavity ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 585.67pt, "stability. For more conservative approach, the designer could ensure a cavity angle below 14° (for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 600.07pt, "R1234yf) as this resulted in stability equivalent to a cylindrical cavity. Furthermore, this allows for a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 614.59pt, "simpler design process as the rewetting area may be calculated using the cavity radius only. ")
  #line-at(249.65pt, 287.69pt, "(a) ")
  #line-at(339.79pt, 287.33pt, "(b) ")
]

