#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-29.png", width: 418.5pt), 88pt, 80.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(512.26pt, 790.176pt, "29 ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(77.664pt, 278.69pt, "Figure 19: Scatter plot of the bubble departure diameter for (a) cylindrical and (b) conical cavities of various cavity depths ")
  #line-at(280.37pt, 289.73pt, "and radii. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#44546a"))
  #line-at(79.224pt, 512.47pt, "Figure 20: Scatter plot of the equivalent diameter of the bubble remaining attached to the surface after departure for (a) ")
  #line-at(174.62pt, 523.51pt, "cylindrical and (b) conical cavities of various cavity depths and radii. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 15.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#2f5496"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 553.15pt, "4")
  #line-at(93.624pt, 553.15pt, "Conclusions  ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 569.35pt, "This study numerically investigated the effect of cylindrical and conical cavity geometries on the bubble ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 583.87pt, "dynamics associated with the nucleate pool boiling of R1234yf. At a fixed input heat flux of 28 kW/m")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.18pt, 579.91pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(520.66pt, 583.87pt, ", ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 598.39pt, "the cavity radius was varied between 50 μm and 400 μm and the cavity depth between 100 μm and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 612.79pt, "1000 μm. The cavity angle of the conical cavities resulted from the choice in cavity radius and depth. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 627.34pt, "The following conclusions were made from the single bubble dynamics:  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 650.5pt, "The equivalent diameter at departure did not vary for cavities with radii below a critical radius ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 664.9pt, "of 120 μm (approximately one sixth of the capillary length). However, the equivalent diameter ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 679.42pt, "at departure increased linearly with increasing cavity radius above this critical radius. Due to ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 693.94pt, "the necking process before bubble departure, the bubble base receded along the surface, ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 708.34pt, "reaching a minimum radius equal to the respective cavity radius for cylindrical cavities. ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 722.86pt, "Therefore, the area of surface rewetting was a function of cavity radius only. Smaller cavity ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 737.38pt, "radii enabled more rewetting, while a radius larger than the peak bubble base radius of 275 ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 751.896pt, "μm experienced no rewetting during bubble detachment.  ")
]

// arXiv source page 30
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-30.png", width: 4pt), 90.5pt, 77.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(512.26pt, 790.176pt, "30 ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 83.04pt, "The cavity geometry led to variations in bubble growth time due to differences in the initial ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 97.58pt, "thermal boundary layer thickness. This variation is assumed to become negligible for systems ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 111.98pt, "with low Jakob numbers at quasi-steady state conditions.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 127.1pt, "The cavity angle of conical cavities was found to have a negligible effect on the bubble ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 141.62pt, "departure diameter for angles less than a quarter of the contact angle. These conical cavities ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 156.14pt, "also had similar vapour retention potential to cylindrical cavities. As the angle was increased ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 170.54pt, "further, the bubble departure diameter increased, with the amount of vapour remaining in ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 185.06pt, "the cavity after departure decreasing. For cavity angles large than 60°, complete vapour ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 199.58pt, "departure was observed. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 222.02pt, "When translating the fundamental knowledge obtained for single cavities to cavity array design, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 236.54pt, "following recommendations were made: ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 259.58pt, "Cylindrical cavities are recommended for more stable and predictable heat transfer rates as ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 274.13pt, "they have a higher likelihood of encouraging nucleation.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 289.25pt, "Smaller cavity radii are favourable for subcooled conditions where rewetting heat transfer is ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 303.65pt, "most significant. Furthermore, smaller cavity radii are preferable as they allow for greater ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 318.17pt, "nucleation site density while resulting in the same bubble departure diameter as larger cavity ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 332.69pt, "radii. ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 347.81pt, "Larger cavity radii are recommended for high superheat conditions as they typically resulted ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 362.21pt, "in larger bubbles, with more bubble surface area remaining after detachment (dependent on ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 376.73pt, "cavity angle for conical cavities), which could enhance bubble growth if the thermal boundary ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 391.25pt, "layer was sufficiently developed.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 406.37pt, "For cavities with radii below the critical radius of 120 μm, a negligible variation in the resulting ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 420.77pt, "bubble dynamics was found and so less stringent manufacturing tolerances may be utilised, ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 435.29pt, "potentially saving on costs.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 450.43pt, "It was found that the cavity depth was not an important design parameter for cylindrical ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 464.83pt, "cavities but should be accounted for when using conical cavities as it affected the departure ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 479.35pt, "time as well as stability.  ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 494.47pt, "Conical cavity array designs are more complicated as the larger cavity angles may allow for ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 508.99pt, "larger bubble departure diameters at the cost of nucleation site density as well as cavity ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 523.39pt, "stability, which both affects the overall heat transfer rate and longevity of the system.  ")
